course: dictionary
lesson: 13715
title: Instill: putting ideas into minds
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To instill something means to put an idea, feeling or habit into someone's mind slowly, so that it stays there. It is not done in one moment. Parents, teachers and coaches do it again and again.
explain: «Mum instilled good manners in me when I was little.» Good manners did not arrive in one day. She taught them over and over until they became a habit that stayed.
explain: «Coach Kim instilled teamwork in the team.» Look at the clues: a coach is teaching the players, and the result is something that stays in their minds. Ask yourself: is something being put into someone's mind, and is it meant to last?
explain: The word has a rare medical meaning too: to put liquid in drop by drop, like eye drops. In a sentence about feelings, habits or ideas, the first meaning is the one to use.
fact: Instill means to slowly put an idea, feeling or habit into someone's mind so it stays.
fact: The UK spelling is instil. The US spelling is instill. Both are correct in their own places.
fact: Phrases to know: instil confidence, instil fear, instil a love of reading.
fact: Instil is often followed by in or into: instil kindness in a child.
fact: The rare medical meaning is to put liquid in drop by drop, like eye drops.
exercise: «The loud dog instilled fear in the postman.» What was put into the postman's mind? | The dog's name | *Fear | A sunny day | A bone
exercise: «Our teacher instils a love of reading in every class.» Which word is closest in meaning to instils? | *Teaches slowly | Forgets | Breaks | Measures
exercise: «The grandad instilled kindness in his grandchildren.» Find and copy one word that is a form of instil. | *instilled | grandad | kindness | grandchildren
test: «Mum's kind words instilled confidence in me.» What was instilled? | Kind words that were thrown away | *Confidence, put slowly into my mind so it stays | Confidence | Mum's car keys
test: Which is the US spelling of the verb? | instyll | instil | *instill | instile
exam: «The story instilled a love of reading in Maya.» What does instilled mean here? | Sent her to sleep | Gave her a book to read today | Made her dislike books | *Slowly put a love of reading into her mind so it stays
